  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Patchy morning fog, high humidity, hot \ntemperatures, and daily thunderstorm chances continue through early \nnext week. \n\nMore of the same when it comes to the forecast Friday into the \nupcoming weekend. Patchy morning fog gives way to high humidity, hot \ntemperatures, and late day thunderstorm chances. This is largely due \nin part to blocking high pressure offshore and continued waves of \nupper level energy pivoting through. The nearest front is back \ntoward Chicago with little movement eastward in the coming days due \nblocking high pressure offshore. \n\nFor today, expect similar conditions to what we saw Thursday \nalthough the severe threat will be higher. A dry and muggy start \nwith some fog giving way to mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies in \nthe afternoon. The combination of the heat and humidity with boost \nsurface based CAPE values above 2000 j/kg+ with DCAPE values \nremaining around or less than 1000 j/kg. 0-6 km shear values will \nsit between 25-30 kts with mid level lapse rates between 6-7.5 C/km \nwhich would allow for higher storm coverage and more progressive \nmovement from west to east across the region. Storms will initiate \nin the lee of the Alleghenies around 18-20z/2-4pm before advancing \neastward toward the metros toward the late afternoon and evening \nrush hours commute 21-00z/5-8pm as the impulse works through. Storms \ncould fire ahead of the shortwave via the bay/river breeze similar \nto what we had on Thursday. With all that saId, SPC has upgraded the \nareas near and along the I-95 (DC/NORTHERN VA burbs northeast \ninto central MD) into a Slight Risk (Level 2 out of 5) for \nsevere weather this afternoon and evening. The main threats with\nstorms look to be damaging winds (15% probabilities) and heavy \nrainfall although isolated large hail cannot be ruled out due to\nthe higher mid-level lapse rates. \n\nSimilar conditions are expected Saturday as another potent shortwave \npasses through. Instability will remain high as a result of the high \nheat and humidity. Shear values will sit around 30 kts perhaps \nincreasing to 35 kts as the subtropical jet stream begins to \nstrengthen. This could yield a more organized severe weather threat \nwith aligns with current learning machine guidance which suggest 15-\n30 percent probabilities for damaging wind gusts Saturday afternoon \nand evening. With that said, guidance continues to focus on areas \nalong and north of I-66/US-50 for both Friday and Saturday. \n\nConvection begins to wane a bit Sunday into Monday given quasi-zonal \nflow aloft. It will be short-lived with storm chances increasing \nonce again Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday as a series of shortwave \ndisturbances passing through. By Wednesday and Thursday, a front \ntries to drop into the region potentially bringing a change to the \ncurrent pattern. \n\nOutside of the storm chances expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s with heat indices east of the BLue Ridge up around 100 degrees. \nThe hottest days look to be Sunday through Tuesday given the \nstrengthened subtropical ridge over the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front could arrive late next week and into \nthe weekend bringing relief from the heat and humidity.  \n\nWhile this is very far out in the forecast period, there is some \nsignal that a more potent frontal system could push through the \nregion late in the week. The details are murky at this stage of the \nforecast, with timing very uncertain, as well as potential impacts. \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance is generally between \nThursday/Friday right now, with improvement in the weather pattern \npossible into the upcoming weekend. Any decent frontal passage in \nthe summertime should be watched closely for potential severe \nweather, so we will keep monitoring this in the coming days to see \nif that potential is there, and try to narrow down the timeframe a \nbit more.",
